 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "sola-non-est",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Paradox",
   "quote": "relicta sola ... sola non est",
   "trans": "left alone ... is not alone",
   "body": "Apuleius plants a paradox: Psyche is <em>relicta sola</em> (\"left alone\") and yet, hounded by the <em>Furiae</em>, <em>sola non est</em> (\"is not alone\"). Her guilt peoples the empty room.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The paradox <em>relicta sola ... sola non est</em> (‘left alone ... is not alone’) dramatises Psyche’s psychological torment: even in solitude she is crowded by the avenging Furies of her own conscience.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "aestu-pelagi",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Simile",
   "quote": "aestu pelagi simile ... fluctuat",
   "trans": "wavers like the swell of the sea",
   "body": "Her wavering is compared to the <em>aestus pelagi</em>, the swell of the open sea, and the verb <em>fluctuat</em> (\"surges, billows\") keeps the water-image alive. Her emotions rise and fall like waves.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The sea-simile <em>aestu pelagi simile ... fluctuat</em> renders Psyche’s indecision physical: like the swell of the sea she heaves back and forth, the verb <em>fluctuat</em> sustaining the watery metaphor.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "festinat-differt",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Asyndeton",
   "quote": "festinat differt, audet trepidat, diffidit irascitur",
   "trans": "she hurries, delays, dares, panics, despairs, rages",
   "body": "Six present-tense verbs in three clipped, conjunction-less pairs (<em>asyndeton</em>), each pair an opposite. The breathless rhythm enacts her flickering, contradictory feelings — we feel her mind racing.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The asyndetic tricolon of antithetical verbs (<em>festinat differt, audet trepidat, diffidit irascitur</em>) mimics the speed and instability of Psyche’s emotions; the absence of connectives makes each impulse cancel the last.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "odit-diligit",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Antithesis",
   "quote": "in eodem corpore odit bestiam, diligit maritum",
   "trans": "in the same body she hates the beast, loves the husband",
   "body": "The climax of her division: <em>in eodem corpore</em> (\"in one and the same person\") she both <em>odit</em> and <em>diligit</em>. <em>Bestiam</em> (the monster her sisters described) is balanced against <em>maritum</em> (the husband she loves).",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The antithesis <em>odit bestiam, diligit maritum</em>, framed by <em>in eodem corpore</em>, crystallises Psyche’s inner conflict: a single person holds two irreconcilable feelings for the same man.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "vespera-trahente",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Personification",
   "quote": "vespera ... noctem trahente",
   "trans": "evening drawing on the night",
   "body": "In the ablative absolute the evening itself \"drags on\" the night (<em>noctem trahente</em>). Time is personified and made to move slowly, drawing out the suspense before the deed.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The ablative absolute <em>vespera ... noctem trahente</em> personifies the evening as slowly hauling night behind it, stretching the moment and building tension before the attempt.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
